.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-admin-catalog-tokens - Every token configured in this environment, on chains not in service too


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 admin catalog tokens [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
List every token this gateway is configured with, whole rows, with the EIP-712 domain
(eip712_name, eip712_version) the gateway signs against. 'rail0 tokens' stops at the
chains in service.

.PP
A row names its chain by the catalogue's own reference (blockchain_id); the command adds
chain_id and chain_slug, read from 'rail0 admin catalog chains', so a row can be read by
itself.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for tokens


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 admin catalog tokens --pretty
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-admin-catalog(1)\fP
